---
import Callout from '../Callout.astro';
import AgentLoop from '../diagrams/AgentLoop.astro';
---

<div class="sec" id="s1">
  <div class="num">01</div>
  <h2>How to think about AI tools</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    A chatbot answers. An agent works. That is the whole idea in six words.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you type into Claude and it replies, that is a chat. You ask, it
    answers. Then it is up to you to go and do something with the answer. Ask it
    for a recipe and you get a recipe. Ask it for an email and you get an email.
    Claude gives you words, and the doing is still yours.
  </p>
  <p>
    An agent is Claude with hands. You point it at a folder on your computer and
    it can read the files in there, edit them, run commands (the typed
    instructions a computer understands) and then check whether the result
    worked. If something fails, it reads the error and tries again. You are
    still in charge, because it shows you what it plans to do and you approve or
    reject each change.
  </p>
  <div class="card" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px">
    <AgentLoop />
    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: var(--color-9ee6b5)">
      The loop: you write a prompt, the agent works on your files, you review
      what it did. Accept it or send it back with a comment. Repeat.
    </p>
  </div>
  <Callout title={'The review step is the whole job'}
    ><svg
      slot="icon"
      width="22"
      height="22"
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      fill="none"
      stroke="var(--color-b8ffd0)"
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
      ><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><path d="M12 16v-4"></path><path
        d="M12 8h.01"></path></svg
    ><p>
      Engineers spend most of their time reading, not typing. With an agent,
      that is now your job too. You do not need to understand every line. You
      need to check that what it says it did matches what you asked for.
    </p></Callout
  >
  <p>
    The closest thing I can compare it to is briefing a contractor. You are not
    typing into a chat box, you are handing someone a job. A contractor you have
    briefed badly does not stop and ring you. They guess, and they build the
    guess. Agents are the same. If your brief leaves a question open, the agent
    will not ask it. It will pick an answer and keep going. That is why the next
    section is about the brief.
  </p>
</div>
